  <h2><a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog/2020/11/24/">You might not ne=
ed machine learning</a></h2>
  <time datetime=3D"2020-11-24">
    November 24, 2020
  </time>
  <div class=3D"print-only url">
    nullprogram.com/blog/2020/11/24/
  </div>

  <p><em>This article was discussed <a href=3D"https://news.ycombinator.com=
/item?id=3D25196574">on Hacker News</a>.</em></p>

<p>Machine learning is a trendy topic, so naturally it=E2=80=99s often used=
 for
inappropriate purposes where a simpler, more efficient, and more reliable
solution suffices. The other day I saw an illustrative and fun example of
this: <a href=3D"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3D-sg-GgoFCP0">Neural Netw=
ork Cars and Genetic Algorithms</a>. The video
demonstrates 2D cars driven by a neural network with weights determined by
a generic algorithm. However, the entire scheme can be replaced by a
first-degree polynomial without any loss in capability. The machine
learning part is overkill.</p>

<p><a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/video/?v=3Dracetrack"><img src=3D"htt=
ps://nullprogram.com/img/screenshot/racetrack.jpg" alt=3D"" style=3D"image-=
rendering: pixelated;"></a></p>

<!--more-->

<p>Above demonstrates my implementation using a polynomial to drive the car=
s.
My wife drew the background. There=E2=80=99s no path-finding; these cars ar=
e just
feeling their way along the track, =E2=80=9Cfollowing the rails=E2=80=9D so=
 to speak.</p>

<p>My intention is not to pick on this project in particular. The likely
motivation in the first place was a desire to apply a neural network to
<em>something</em>. Many of my own projects are little more than a vehicle =
to try
something new, so I can sympathize. Though a professional setting is
different, where machine learning should be viewed with a more skeptical
eye than it=E2=80=99s usually given. For instance, don=E2=80=99t use active=
 learning to
select sample distribution when a <a href=3D"http://extremelearning.com.au/=
unreasonable-effectiveness-of-quasirandom-sequences/">quasirandom sequence<=
/a> will do.</p>

<p>In the video, the car has a limited turn radius, and minimum and maximum
speeds. (I=E2=80=99ve retained these contraints in my own simulation.) Ther=
e are
five sensors =E2=80=94 forward, forward-diagonals, and sides =E2=80=94 each=
 sensing the
distance to the nearest wall. These are fed into a 3-layer neural network,
and the outputs determine throttle and steering. Sounds pretty cool!</p>

<p><img src=3D"https://nullprogram.com/img/diagram/racecar.svg" alt=3D""></=
p>

<p>A key feature of neural networks is that the outputs are a nonlinear
function of the inputs. However, steering a 2D car is simple enough that
<strong>a linear function is more than sufficient</strong>, and neural netw=
orks are
unnecessary. Here are my equations:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>steering =3D C0*input1 - C0*input3
throttle =3D C1*input2
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>I only need three of the original inputs =E2=80=94 forward for throttle,=
 and
diagonals for steering =E2=80=94 and the driver has just two parameters, <c=
ode class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C0</code> and
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">C1</code>, the polynom=
ial coefficients. Optimal values depend on the track
layout and car configuration, but for my simulation, most values above 0
and below 1 are good enough in most cases. It=E2=80=99s less a matter of cr=
ashing
and more about navigating the course quickly.</p>

<p>The lengths of the red lines below are the driver=E2=80=99s three inputs=
:</p>

<video src=3D"https://nullprogram.com/vid/racecar.mp4" width=3D"530" height=
=3D"330" loop=3D"" muted=3D"" autoplay=3D"" controls=3D"">
</video>

<p>These polynomials are obviously much faster than a neural network, but
they=E2=80=99re also easy to understand and debug. I can confidently reason=
 about
the entire range of possible inputs rather than worry about a trained
neural network <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.06638">responding stra=
ngely</a> to untested inputs.</p>

<p>Instead of doing anything fancy, my program generates the coefficients a=
t
random to explore the space. If I wanted to generate a good driver for a
course, I=E2=80=99d run a few thousand of these and pick the coefficients t=
hat
complete the course in the shortest time. For instance, these coefficients
make for a fast, capable driver for the course featured at the top of the
article:</p>

<div class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge"><div class=3D"highlight=
"><pre class=3D"highlight"><code>C0 =3D 0.896336973, C1 =3D 0.0354805067
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Many constants can complete the track, but some will be faster than
others. If I was developing a racing game using this as the AI, I=E2=80=99d=
 not
just pick constants that successfully complete the track, but the ones
that do it quickly. Here=E2=80=99s what the spread can look like:</p>

<video src=3D"https://nullprogram.com/vid/racecars.mp4" width=3D"530" heigh=
t=3D"330" loop=3D"" muted=3D"" autoplay=3D"" controls=3D"">
</video>

<p>If you want to play around with this yourself, here=E2=80=99s my C sourc=
e code
that implements this driving AI and <a href=3D"https://nullprogram.com/blog=
/2017/11/03/">generates the videos and images
above</a>:</p>

<p><strong><a href=3D"https://github.com/skeeto/scratch/blob/master/aidrive=
rs/aidrivers.c">aidrivers.c</a></strong></p>

<p>Racetracks are just images drawn in your favorite image editing program
using the colors documented in the source header.</p>



